Date: Sat, 1 May 1993 14:58:23 -0700 (PDT)
From: Scott C DeLancey <delancey@darkwing.uoregon.edu>
Subject: LANG: Greenberg (was PUBS: SciAm)

(This is lurching off-topic; if anybody wants to pursue it further it
should probably be by e-mail)

Michael Clive Price asks:

> Scott DeLancey says (paraphrasing) that Greenberg's work on AmerIndian
> linguistic classification is crap.  Greenberg makes the point, which
> I've not seen disputed, that he faced the same criticisms for his work
> on African language classifications - and after 30+ years of sound and
> fury his results are now accepted.  This make Greenberg's case more
> credible, to me.  Is Greenberg lying about his accomplishments?

The two cases are different in several ways--e.g. that Greenberg was
trained as an African languge specialist, but doesn't show any sign of
knowing much of anything about American languages.  The most significant,
IMO, though the least talked-about (at least publically) is this:  45 years
ago, when he was doing his African work, he was a young, ambitious scholar,
just out of grad school, anxious to make his rep.  And he did his work
carefully and accurately, and he got results that have stood up.  15 years
ago, when he was doing the American stuff, he was an established senior
scholar with a substantial reputation which, alas, he may have taken  (and
still takes) a little more seriously than is good for him.
    The "my African work has proved out" argument is one he brings up
against critics who say that his methods are bogus from the start.  There
are problems with the methodology, but I think he's right that the success
of his African work shows that it has potential.  The real problem with
his American stuff is that it's so goddam sloppy,  Every expert in some
American family who looks at Greenberg's stuff finds long lists of
inaccurate, misanalyzed, or downright spurious data.  He and his supporters
(who by this point probably could be counted on two hands, maybe on one)
say that the method irons out bad data, but there's so much bad data
that it's impossible to tell if there's anything there.
     Another problem is that with the American stuff he seems to have
had a preconceived idea of how it would come out right from the beginning.
People who've combed through his data find that the results he announces
are the ones he was expecting, but not always necessarily the ones that
the data actually imply.


Scott DeLancey



