Subject: PHIL: Metaphysics vs. Epistemology, Noumena vs. Phenomena

On Fri, 30 Apr 1993, Derek Zahn wrote:

> AI is one of those places where the Aristotlean tradition
> still has serious followers (well, in the last half-decade
> or so more and more AI'ers have abandoned that ship).
> 
> As was discussed here a few months ago, BTW, that belief in
> an A "perfect system of categories and deductions" also goes
> by the name "Objectivism" [see Lakoff's _Women, Fire, and
> Dangerous Things_, which surprisingly seems to be a rather
> nice complement to Korzybski, from what I've seen so far].
> That Objectivism is a premise of Rand's (broader?)
> Objectivism.

I don't think it goes by that name voluntarily.  "Objectivists" is
Lakoff's epithet for his intellectual enemies (who in many ways are
a pretty motley crew to be all painted with one label) but I'm not
aware that any of them call themselves anything like that.

But you're right about the congruence between Lakoff's stuff and
Korzybski.  Coincidental, in one sense; Lakoff is not a shy guy,
and I'm sure if he saw any link between what he's doing and GS (which
I suspect he knows little or nothing about) he wouldn't hesitate
to mention & cite it.  But not coincidental, in another, and maybe not
all that surprising; Lakoff is trying to present a corrective to orthodox 
ideas about semantics and cognition (his "objectivism") which are grounded 
in the same folk psychology as is Aristotle.


Scott DeLancey



