From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Subject: metaphysics vs epistemology

Brillant posts by Eric Raymond!  Tim, don't burn him.... :-)

> That insight doesn't become mathematical *knowledge* until it's
> deductively linked to the premises of a formal system.

Sounds like a definition.

> The fact that mathematical discover often *feels* empirical is an
> interesting datum of creative psychology.  But it's not in itself 
> philosophically significant.

Really?  I thought feelings were of primal importance to neo-pagans
(they are to me).  Hind- and fore- brain synergy and all that.:-) :-) 
Many mathematicians may officially subscribe to formalism, but in their
hearts (read: hindbrain) they know they are really Platonists.  I call
them operational-platonists.....  Personally I have no problems
regarding maths as a tautologous platonic edifice, some bits of which
are also useful.  As Eric says an operationalist draws on data
eclectically, using deduction and induction.

Tim Starr, IMHO, has missed the point about Aristotle and induction. 
Aristotle may have used induction in ethics (I don't know nor care) but
he certainly didn't use induction in physics.  His 'theories' about
dynamics were just full of observational howlers and hypotheses about
objects finding their 'natural' places etc.  Compared with Archimedes
Aristotle was a Platonist.  Compared with Plato Aristotle was an
inductionist.  It took Archimedes, Galileo, Newton and others to break
the legacy of Platonism which Aristotle contributed to.
 
Eric's mentions the differences between Euro-continental and
Anglo-American philosophy.  Certainly that's spot on.  Compare the way
Newton and Galileo were treated.
 
> Operationalism demands that you ground reasoning in the empirical, but
> has no problem with the use of deductive universals as heuristics or
> aids to hypothesis formation.

I find the operational approach very useful to get a handle on slippery
concepts and have used it for years.  Instead of worrying about the
'true' meaning of a concept ask what observational or empirical
implications it has.  In operational terms absolute morals are
meaningless, since no experiment can detect them?  Operationalists have
to conclude absolute morals, like absolute space, do not exist = the
concept is meaningless.  It's a very useful touchstone to apply to
concepts.

