Interesting idea, but I think it hightly unlikely due to the harsh subsis-
tence nature of life in this area back then.  Most of the evidence I have
seen points to the place being a ritual-only center, sort of like a magico-
religious version of the county fairgrounds.  Whatever the purpose it is 
certainly impressive.  I was out there just last summer.  Ver interesting.
Also went to Chaco Canyon, Cliff Palace, Canyon de Chelly, etc.  Quite 
a trip.  When I went to Arcosanti last fall, I asked the tour guide if
Anasazi architecture had played a part in Arcosanti's design, but the answer
was "no".  Personally I just don't buy it.  There are far too many similar-
ities.  I think it's just that the "party line" of Arcosanti is that Paolo
is just such an original genius, that nothing of his ideas ever came from
anywhere but his own imagination.  Plllllbbbb...  That is what bugged me
about the place.  However "extropian" the idea of a totally intentional
community, with the idea of becoming fully self-sufficient, and absent of
the usual governmental presence we are currently infested with may sound,
Arcosanti sure isn't It.  I think it started out that way, but those people
treat Soleri like some sort of god.  Kind of a shame really.  I think that
is a large part of the reason why after 20+ years, the place is still less
that 5% finished.  No one can tolerate being treated like slave labor to
His Royal Highness long enough to stick it out there.  Oh well, so much for
Tanelorn  ;)
