Subject: LANG: Greenberg (was PUBS: SciAm)

On Sun, 2 May 1993, Michael Clive Price wrote:

> Thanks Scott for the answer about Greenberg.  So his methodology is
> sound - but his research isn't/may not be.  Okay, I'll buy that. 

Since I'm kind of speaking for the profession here, a caveat--the
"mass comparison" methodology is the subject of a lot of controversy, 
some of it legitimate.  I personally think it has potential value,
but only if applied very carefully.  For a project like _Language in
the Americas_ that would mean you'd need a large team of experts to
do it right.
     There isn't, unfortunately, any "may" any longer about the 
unsoundness of his work.  (I can send you lots of references if
you're interested in details).  This is sad; he's a brilliant scholar,
a hard worker, and a very nice guy.  But _Language in the Americas_
is a disaster.

> I hope Scott doesn't mind me keeping this on-line

Hey, I can talk about this stuff for days, it just seems a bit off-topic
for Extropians.

> but I know we have
> a large number of philologists on-list and I've one more question.
> 
> How does the genetic and linguistic evidence tie together?

Another controversial question.  Cavalli-Sforza and others have been
doing a lot of work trying to correlate genetic with linguistic 
relationships and diversity.  He had an article in SA a year or two
ago about it.

>  I ask
> because SciAm published a putative language tree (for the whole of
> humanity) which suggested convergence about (say) 12,000+ years ago, but
> the corresponding genetic tree (showing the times that different races
> diverged) had the same structure but the time scales were about an order
> of magnitude or so longer.  Seems to me that either the origins of
> language must lie much further back (was word-turnover much lower in the
> early days?) or the genetic data is skewed.  Or was I reading too much
> into the similarities?

What article was this?  A 12,000 BP date for linguistic divergence is
absolutely bogus.  Any date suggested for an original undifferentiated
human language would have to be pure guesswork, but the absolute minimum
possible would be 50,000 BP.  (I'd guess that most people would set the 
minimum plausible date to be farther back than that).  The Australian 
aborigines have been there for at least 40,000.  12,000 is Greenberg's (and
many others') date for the settlement of the New World, so by his reckoning 
that's the time when Amerind languages began to diverge (I, with a lot of
other linguists and archaeologists, would put this 5-10,000 years farther 
back).  But there would already have been tens of millenia (at least) of
divergence before that to get the proto-Amerinds all the way to Siberia in the
first place.


