From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Subject: metaphysics vs epistemology

> I find the operational approach very useful to get a handle on slippery
> concepts and have used it for years.  Instead of worrying about the
> 'true' meaning of a concept ask what observational or empirical
> implications it has.  In operational terms absolute morals are
> meaningless, since no experiment can detect them?  Operationalists have
> to conclude absolute morals, like absolute space, do not exist = the
> concept is meaningless.  It's a very useful touchstone to apply to
> concepts.

> Mike Price                         price@price.demon.co.uk

In the vast majority of cases, the moral relativists are clearly right,
and if an action is described as "immoral" then this just demonstrates a
cultural bias, or the views of a particular bunch of statists. E.g., the
idea that it is "wrong" to take certain drugs, or for private citizens
to own specific weapons, or to practise cannibalism.

But what about the instance of randomly directed violence -- say, lethal
violence -- initiated against a totally innocent third party? This might be
carried out by private individuals, terrorists, or agents of a totalitarian
regime (last but by no means least!). Is it morally right to have innocent
sentient lifeforms, human beings for instance, tortured and murdered simply
because they happened to be where they were, or because of the circumstances
of their birth? 

It's true that the universe could care less about what goes on. Neither is
there any way to demonstrate operationally whether some 'God' frowns upon
certain types of behaviour. But even if we could somehow know that a 'God'
/ 'Nature' or the universe wanted lifeforms to behave a certain way, and that
to go against this code was "immoral", this would be a crazy strategy for us.
It makes sense for humans to do whatever is best for themselves collectively
(and personally of course!). To ignore this and do what was 'good for God' or
'right for the Earth' would be tantamount to exploitation of the human race
by another entity, probably imaginary. A non-optimal strategy, although 
advocated by some.

The only possible value of "morality" is as a set of guidelines to demonstrate
when the good of the individual conflicts with the collective good of the
species. (And this 'conflict' is really greatly overrated, in any case.) If
we assume that it is 'right' to allow anyone to murder other people at random
with impunity, would this be to the collective benefit of the human race? If
another race of beings inhabiting part of the Andromeda Galaxy had a
temporary mental breakdown and made the same assumption, isn't it just as
predictable that they'd later regret it?

So for the experiment, we begin by making a few observations. Mortality rates
are measured, and representative samples of the population are asked a few
questions -- how much do they enjoy life, what are their hopes and fears for
the future, do they feel that life is very stressful etc. Then, with immediate
effect, all penalties for murder are suddenly rescinded (the people who were
interviewed are those who were successfully kept in the dark over the fact
that this was going to happen).

People are constantly reminded that to kill their fellow man is perfectly
correct; "You've got to let your true feelings out and know yourself.
Besides, just think of the thrill of it all." After a year has passed, the
same measurements are taken, questions asked, and compared with the original.

So I think there is a distinction between the existence and non-existence of
certain absolute morals, specifically the random initiation of lethal force. 
Once the force is greatly scaled down it's obviously impossible to make any
absolute judgement about it -- if you blow at someone / give them a playful
tap / push them to the ground / inflict very minor injury.... But in the most
extreme case of murder, it appears that all intelligent sentient life would
arrive at the same conclusion whatever their culture and customs: to allow it
is to the grave collective disadvantage of their species.

Steve Whitrow                           sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk

