  I have received many responses to my post questioning David Freidman's
post about the "rational" basis for racial discrimination.  They have
invariably been personal anecdotes.  The writers then extrapolate
the *personal* experiences that they have had onto a whole class (race)
of people.
 
  I have read that if we had a "good ID system" that these problems would
go away.  Or if we had a good database full of "these people" or their
vehicles we would only need to pull over this "questionable" person once.
But still, the initial action is based *entirely* upon misinformation
and wrongful assumptions (many times).
 
  I can't compare a few anecdotal experiences to the treatment of people of
color in this country (US).  Not to mention that these experiences are still
and admittedly not comparable.  The whites that have been 'mistreated' were
not treated the same way that a person of color would have been given the
inverse situation.  Being lovingly nudged out of a neighborhood and having
your body thrown against a cop car, legs spread, searched from head to toe,
and handcuffed are not experiences that can really be compared.  I would hope
that the thinkers on this list can see the difference.
 
  I hesitated posting these responses because I was unsure as to whether it
was appropriate in Extropians.  But I think it is.  People who are thinking
far enough ahead to have a 'new' justice system online and who speak of
Platonic Ideals (critical or supportive) should have thought about race in a
critical and futuristic way.  Many of the ideas expressed on the list appear
more reminescent of 17th century thought.  Or 21st Century thought in South
Afrika. (I hear they have a "good" ID system)
 
